\section{Look Through a Memory Merge Only When Answers Agree}

A load can have a known value even when different writes reach it
on different paths. Consider a private array with whole-cell reads
and writes, fixed in-bounds indices, and no interfering threads.
For this restricted query the memory graph is acyclic, and its
incoming states conservatively include all possible paths. Given a
cell and a state, return a constant only if every represented path
guarantees that constant; otherwise return \emph{unknown}. The
pass must separately establish that the load itself has no observable
effect and may be replaced.

\begin{sudocode}
\Require State $M$, queried cell $i$
\If{$M$ is a store to cell $j$}
  \If{$i=j$}
    \State \Return constant of the stored value, or unknown
  \EndIf
  \To{A disjoint store leaves the queried cell untouched.}
  \State \Return $\operatorname{query}(\operatorname{prior}(M),i)$
\ElsIf{$M$ is a merge}
  \To{One agreeing predecessor is not enough.}
  \Let{V}{\{\operatorname{query}(p,i):p\in\operatorname{inputs}(M)\}}
  \State \Return the sole constant if $V$ is a singleton constant
\EndIf
\State \Return unknown
\end{sudocode}

Suppose the left arm writes $A[2]=13$ and then $A[0]=99$.
The right arm writes $A[2]=13$ and then $A[4]=7$.
Querying cell $2$ at the merge skips the last write in each arm
and gets $13$ from both inputs, so the answer is $13$. Neither
of the two supplying writes dominates the load. Querying cell
$0$ instead finds $99$ on the left and no known value on the
right. That result must be unknown, even if a concrete test
with an initial $A[0]=99$ happens to return $99$ on both paths.

The two cell queries in Figure~\ref{fig:visual-205} traverse the same memory merge but yield different kinds of answers.

